        "tldr": "Basis Vectors",
        "howItWorks": "## Basis Vectors\nThe computational basis vectors |0> and |1> form the coordinate axes of qubit state space, spanning all superpositions\n- Basis vectors are fundamental, linearly independent vectors that span a vector space.\n- For a single qubit, the computational basis consists of $|0\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $|1\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n- Any quantum state can be expressed as a unique linear combination of basis vectors, with complex amplitudes as coefficients.\n- Basis vectors in quantum mechanics are typically orthonormal, meaning they are perpendicular and have unit length.\n- Different bases (e.g., computational, Hadamard) provide different 'coordinate systems' for the same quantum state space.\n- The choice of basis is critical for defining measurement outcomes and understanding quantum transformations.\n\n\n## Why This Matters\nJust as we describe any location on a map using fundamental directions like North/South and East/West, we describe any quantum state as a combination of fundamental 'basis' states. For a single qubit, these are typically the $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$ states. These basis states are represented mathematically by special vectors called basis vectors.\nUnderstanding basis vectors is crucial because they form the 'coordinate system' for our quantum states. Any qubit state, no matter how complex its superposition, can be expressed as a unique combination of these fundamental building blocks. This provides a structured way to analyze and manipulate quantum information.\nBy the end of this topic, you will understand what basis vectors are, how they define a vector space, and how any quantum state can be written as a linear combination of these fundamental states.\n\n\n## Core Intuition\nImagine you're trying to describe any color. You don't need an infinite list of colors; you can describe almost any color as a mix of a few 'primary' colors (like red, green, and blue). These primary colors are like basis v",
