        "tldr": "Entanglement Creation",
        "howItWorks": "## Entanglement Creation\nA Hadamard gate followed by a CNOT gate creates a Bell state from two unentangled input qubits\n- Entanglement must be actively engineered using a combination of single-qubit and multi-qubit gates.\n- The standard entanglement circuit consists of a Hadamard gate followed by a CNOT gate.\n- The Hadamard gate creates a single-qubit superposition, which is still a separable state.\n- The CNOT gate distributes the superposition, coupling the qubits and creating the entanglement.\n- Starting with |00â©, the H+CNOT circuit generates the Bell state |Î¦âºâ©.\n- By varying the input basis states, the same circuit can generate all four Bell states.\n- It is mathematically impossible to create entanglement from a separable state using only single-qubit gates.\n\n\n## Why This Matters\nWe have studied the Bell states and their remarkable properties, but how do we actually create them? Entanglement does not happen by accident; it must be engineered. In a quantum computer, we generate entanglement by passing unentangled input qubits through a specific sequence of single-qubit and two-qubit gates.\nIn this topic, we will analyze the standard circuit used to create entanglement. We will dissect the two-gate combination that serves as the universal engine for entanglement: the Hadamard gate followed by a CNOT gate. You will learn how to trace the state vector step-by-step through this circuit, watching the transition from a separable state to a maximally entangled state.\nBy the end of this topic, you will be able to build circuits that generate any of the four Bell states from standard computational basis inputs, and you will understand the physical mechanism of how gates distribute quantum information across multiple wires.\n\n\n## Core Intuition\nImagine you have two blank sheets of paper (initialized to $|00\\rangle$). You want to write a secret message that can only be read when both sheets are placed side-by-side. If you write on them independently, they remain separ",
