        "howItWorks": "## Why Errors Matter\nQuantum errors are continuous amplitude and phase rotations, far more complex than classical binary bit flips\n- Physical quantum systems are highly sensitive to environmental noise, leading to decoherence and computational errors.\n- Quantum errors are more complex than classical errors, consisting of both bit flips (X) and phase flips (Z), as well as continuous rotations.\n- The success probability of a quantum circuit decays exponentially as the total gate count increases.\n- Even with a high physical gate fidelity of 99.9%, a circuit with 1,000 gates has only a 36.8% chance of returning an uncorrupted result.\n- The No-Cloning Theorem prevents the direct application of classical repetition codes to quantum states.\n- Current NISQ-era quantum computers are strictly limited in circuit depth due to the compounding nature of physical gate errors.\n- Transitioning to fault-tolerant quantum computing is the only viable path to executing deep, useful quantum algorithms.\n\n\n## Why This Matters\nIn the previous sections, we explored the remarkable theoretical capabilities of quantum algorithms and the physical architectures designed to run them. However, a stark reality separates the elegant mathematical formulations of quantum gates from the physical hardware operating in laboratories today. This reality is defined by quantum noise, environmental decoherence, and imperfect control systems that introduce errors into quantum computations. Understanding why these errors occur and how they compound is the first critical step toward building practical, large-scale quantum computers.\nEvery physical system is subject to noise, but quantum systems are uniquely sensitive due to the fragile nature of superposition and entanglement. Even the slightest interaction with the surrounding environment, such as thermal fluctuations, electromagnetic interference, or material defects, can destroy the delicate quantum states holding our computational data. This phenomenon, known",
